---
import CodeTabs from "../../components/CodeTabs.astro";
import Pager from "../../components/Pager.astro";
import Picker from "../../components/Picker.astro";
import Page from "../../layouts/Page.astro";
---

<Page
  title="Wider gamuts · oklch-picker"
  description="Display P3 and Rec. 2020 as output spaces, not decoration: the chroma slider reaches further and the value is clamped to the space you chose."
>
  <h1>Wider gamuts</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    The picker works in sRGB by default and ships nothing else. Pass
    <code>gamut</code> to work in a wider space instead.
  </p>

  <CodeTabs
    name="gamut"
    snippets={[
      {
        label: "React",
        lang: "tsx",
        code: `import { P3 } from "@oklch-picker/core/gamuts";

<ColourPicker value={colour} onChange={setColour} gamut={P3} />`,
      },
      {
        label: "Vue",
        lang: "vue",
        code: `<script setup>
import { P3 } from "@oklch-picker/core/gamuts";
<\/script>

<template>
  <ColourPicker v-model="colour" :gamut="P3" />
</template>`,
      },
      {
        label: "Svelte",
        lang: "svelte",
        code: `<script>
  import { P3 } from "@oklch-picker/core/gamuts";
<\/script>

<ColourPicker bind:value={colour} gamut={P3} />`,
      },
      {
        label: "Solid",
        lang: "tsx",
        code: `import { P3 } from "@oklch-picker/core/gamuts";

<ColourPicker value={colour()} onChange={setColour} gamut={P3} />`,
      },
      {
        label: "No framework",
        lang: "js",
        code: `import { P3 } from "@oklch-picker/core/gamuts";

// A gamut is an object, so it is a property rather than an attribute.
document.querySelector("oklch-picker").gamut = P3;`,
      },
      {
        label: "Qwik",
        lang: "tsx",
        code: `// An id, not the object. Qwik serialises props to resume a
// component, and a gamut carries a function, so the id crosses
// the boundary where the object cannot.
<ColourPicker value={colour.value} gamut="p3" onChange$={(c) => (colour.value = c)} />`,
      },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    That is the <strong>output</strong> space, not decoration: the chroma slider
    reaches further, the value is clamped to P3 rather than sRGB, and the notice
    only fires once a colour leaves P3 too. Choosing P3 and then still emitting
    an sRGB colour would defeat the point of choosing it.
  </p>

  <h2>Compare them</h2>

  <p>
    The same colour in each space. Drag chroma to the top of its track in each
    and watch how much further it reaches. sRGB stays outlined on the wider
    charts as a reference, so the safe region remains visible.
  </p>

  {/* All three are given the same `references`, which gives them one shared
      vertical scale. Left to their defaults each chart normalises to its own
      peak and fills its own frame, so a wider gamut can draw no taller than a
      narrower one and the three cannot be compared by eye. */}
  <Picker
    value="oklch(0.7 0.2 145)"
    references={["srgb", "p3", "rec2020"]}
    caption="sRGB, the default."
  />
  <Picker
    value="oklch(0.7 0.2 145)"
    gamut="p3"
    references={["srgb", "p3", "rec2020"]}
    caption="Display P3, reaching further into green."
  />
  <Picker
    value="oklch(0.7 0.2 145)"
    gamut="rec2020"
    references={["srgb", "p3", "rec2020"]}
    caption="Rec. 2020, the widest of the three."
  />

  <p>
    Only spaces <em>narrower</em> than the output draw a line. A line for the
    output itself would trace its own boundary, and a wider one would mark
    colours the picker cannot reach. So sRGB draws none, P3 draws sRGB, and
    Rec. 2020 draws both.
  </p>

  <p>
    Override which spaces are outlined with <code>references</code>, or turn the
    lines off entirely with
    <code>parts=&lbrace;&lbrace; gamutLines: false &rbrace;&rbrace;</code>. The
    chart keeps its scale either way, so turning them off does not resize the
    plot.
  </p>

  <Picker
    value="oklch(0.7 0.2 145)"
    gamut="rec2020"
    references={["srgb", "p3", "rec2020"]}
    parts={{ gamutLines: false }}
    caption="Rec. 2020 with the reference lines off."
  />

  <div class="note">
    <p>
      <code>P3</code> and <code>REC2020</code> live behind their own entry point
      on purpose. An app that never imports them never ships the matrices. The
      bundler drops the module statically, so there is no dynamic import and
      nothing async in the render path. Opting in costs a few hundred bytes.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Letting the user switch</h2>

  <p>
    Off by default. Turn it on and the picker renders a small segmented control:
  </p>

  <Picker
    value="oklch(0.7 0.2 145)"
    gamut="srgb"
    gamutChoices={["srgb", "p3", "rec2020"]}
    parts={{ gamutSwitch: true }}
    caption="Switch the output space, and the chroma range changes with it."
  />

  <CodeTabs
    name="switcher"
    snippets={[
      {
        label: "React",
        lang: "tsx",
        code: `const [gamut, setGamut] = useState(SRGB);

<ColourPicker
  value={colour}
  onChange={setColour}
  gamut={gamut}
  onGamutChange={setGamut}
  gamutChoices={[SRGB, P3, REC2020]}
  parts={{ gamutSwitch: true }}
/>`,
      },
      {
        label: "No framework",
        lang: "js",
        code: `import { SRGB } from "@oklch-picker/core";
import { P3, REC2020 } from "@oklch-picker/core/gamuts";

const picker = document.querySelector("oklch-picker");
picker.gamut = SRGB;
picker.gamutChoices = [SRGB, P3, REC2020];
picker.parts = { gamutSwitch: true };

picker.addEventListener("gamutchange", (event) => {
  picker.gamut = event.detail.gamut;
});`,
      },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    <code>gamutChoices</code> defaults to the output gamut plus its references.
    The control hides itself when that leaves only one option, because one
    option is not a choice.
  </p>

  <h2>Notices</h2>

  <p>
    When a colour falls outside the output gamut the picker says so. The wording
    comes from <code>labels</code>, and there are two keys:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong><code>outOfGamut</code></strong> is the sRGB message. It is also
      the fallback for any space with no wording of its own. It defaults to
      <em>"Outside sRGB, the nearest sRGB colour is used."</em>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>outOf:&lt;gamut id&gt;</code></strong> is the message for
      one output space. With <code>gamut=&lbrace;P3&rbrace;</code> the default
      becomes <em>"Outside Display P3, the nearest Display P3 colour is
      used."</em>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Every message names its own space rather than saying "outside what a screen
    can display": P3 is a screen too, so that phrasing was only ever true while
    sRGB was the only option.
  </p>

  <CodeTabs
    name="notices"
    snippets={[
      {
        label: "React",
        lang: "tsx",
        code: `<ColourPicker
  labels={{
    outOfGamut: "Not displayable.",
    "outOf:p3": "Needs a wide-gamut screen.",
  }}
/>`,
      },
      {
        label: "No framework",
        lang: "html",
        code: `<oklch-picker
  labels='{"outOfGamut": "Not displayable.", "outOf:p3": "Needs a wide-gamut screen."}'
></oklch-picker>`,
      },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    Or turn the message off entirely, leaving the maths untouched. The value is
    still clamped, the hatching still shows, only the text goes:
  </p>

  {/* Two pickers on the same out-of-gamut colour. Showing the notice first
      makes the second one legible: without the pair, a lone silent picker
      reads as a missing message rather than a demonstrated setting. */}
  <Picker
    value="oklch(0.7 0.28 145)"
    caption="The notice on, which is the default."
  />

  <Picker
    value="oklch(0.7 0.28 145)"
    parts={{ notice: false }}
    caption="The same colour with parts.notice off. Still clamped, just silent."
  />

  <Pager />
</Page>
